"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 7.8. The surgical findings included a Gleason 4+3 with tertiary pattern 5 (ISUP grade group 3), pT2a stage, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion or lymph node metastasis. The discrepancy between the biopsy (Gleason 4+3, ISUP grade group 2) and the prostatectomy specimen (ISUP grade group 3) is a point of uncertainty.\n\nThe absence of lymph node metastasis is a favorable finding. However, the positive surgical margins and the discrepancy in grading introduce risk. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding future trajectory. Information on postoperative PSA levels and subsequent changes would be crucial to refine this prognosis. The current heuristic horizon suggests a range for recurrence. The fixed estimated horizon is 49.38 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 37.03–61.72 months; its coverage has not been validated."
